      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Sorry about that. Let's get back to the science.\n\nThe pH level can indeed affect the Maillard reaction. The reaction is more pronounced at higher pH levels, which is why acidic ingredients like yeast or citrus juice can enhance the browning process. On the other hand, high pH levels can slow down the reaction, making it less intense.\n\nThe reason for this is that the Maillard reaction involves the breakdown of amino acids and reducing sugars, which are more readily available at lower pH levels. At higher pH levels, these compounds are more likely to be bound to other molecules, making them less reactive.\n\nTo illustrate this, imagine a goblin's treasure chest – at low pH levels, the treasure is more accessible and easier to plunder, whereas at high pH levels, the treasure is more tightly sealed, making it harder to get to. In the context of the Maillard reaction, the pH level can be thought of as the \"lock\" that controls access to the reactants.\n\nIn general, a pH range of 5.5 to 6.5 is considered optimal for the Maillard reaction, which is why many bread recipes include acidic ingredients like butterm",
